---
import Landing from '../layouts/Landing.astro';
import InstallSnippet from '../components/InstallSnippet.astro';
import FlowDiagram from '../components/FlowDiagram.astro';
import LayerTable from '../components/LayerTable.astro';
import TerminalDemo from '../components/TerminalDemo.astro';
import { repoUrl, siteDescription, siteTitle } from '../lib/meta.ts';
---

<Landing title={siteTitle} description={siteDescription}>
	<section class="hero">
		<div class="land-wrap">
			<h1>Temporary, read-only, audited database access for AI coding agents.</h1>
			<p class="tagline">
				No password ever goes into the chat, the agent cannot write, and every query is logged
				with who, why, what, how many rows and how long.
			</p>
			<div class="actions">
				<a class="land-btn land-btn-primary" href="/docs/quickstart/">Quickstart</a>
				<a class="land-btn land-btn-secondary" href={repoUrl}>GitHub</a>
			</div>
			<InstallSnippet command="npm install -g peephole-db" />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="land-section" aria-labelledby="how">
		<div class="land-wrap">
			<h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
			<p class="land-lead">
				You open a lease: <code>peephole open prod --ttl 45m --reason "..."</code>. For that
				time the agent can run <code>SELECT</code>s on your Postgres through five MCP tools.
				Then the door shuts on its own.
			</p>
			<FlowDiagram />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="land-section" aria-labelledby="safe">
		<div class="land-wrap">
			<h2 id="safe">Why it is safe</h2>
			<LayerTable />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="land-section" aria-labelledby="demo">
		<div class="land-wrap">
			<h2 id="demo">In the terminal</h2>
			<p class="land-lead">
				Open a lease, run <code>peephole doctor</code>, then three queries: one works, <code>SELECT *</code> on a table with hidden columns is refused by Postgres, and an <code>UPDATE</code> never reaches it. <code>peephole log</code> shows all three.
			</p>
			<TerminalDemo />
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="land-section" aria-labelledby="why-not">
		<div class="land-wrap">
			<h2 id="why-not">Why not just give the agent the DB URL?</h2>
			<div class="land-prose">
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>The password lands in context and config forever.</strong> It sits in <code>.mcp.json</code>, in the chat transcript, in whatever the client logs. Rotating
					it later means finding every copy.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Nothing expires.</strong> Access granted for one debugging session is still
					there next month.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Writes are possible.</strong> A URL to the application user can <code>UPDATE</code>, <code>DELETE</code> and <code>DROP</code>. One wrong tool call, or
					one row of injected text that reads like an instruction, is enough.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>PII flows into the model.</strong> <code>SELECT * FROM users</code> ships
					every email, phone and token to the model provider, and into the transcript.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Nobody can say afterwards what was read.</strong> No log ties a query to a
					person and a reason.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Peephole replaces the URL with a lease, a role, a parser and an audit line.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="land-section" aria-labelledby="plugin">
		<div class="land-wrap">
			<h2 id="plugin">Claude Code plugin</h2>
			<div class="land-prose">
			<p>
				The plugin bundles the MCP server, so you skip <code>claude mcp add</code>. Two commands
				inside Claude Code, and the tools are there the next time a lease is open. Any other MCP
				client works too: the command is <code>peephole mcp</code> over stdio.
			</p>
			<pre class="land-code"><code>/plugin marketplace add Sajal0208/peephole
/plugin install peephole@peephole</code></pre>
			<p><a href="/docs/plugin/">What the plugin adds on top of the bare MCP server</a></p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</Landing>

<style>
	.hero {
		padding-block: 3.5rem 3rem;
	}
	.hero h1 {
		font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 1.2rem + 3.2vw, 3rem);
		max-width: 20ch;
	}
	.tagline {
		margin-top: 1.25rem;
		max-width: 40rem;
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		color: var(--land-muted);
	}
	.actions {
		display: flex;
		flex-wrap: wrap;
		gap: 0.75rem;
		margin-block: 1.75rem 1.25rem;
	}
	@media (min-width: 50em) {
		.hero {
			padding-block: 5.5rem 4.5rem;
		}
	}
</style>
